    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build succeeds. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I implemented\n\n`bulkSignupWarning` is a per-owner fact fixed at creation time (like `namesakeCount` / `customerCode`), and the acceptance test reads it back via `GET /api/owners/{id}` \u2014 so it needs to be computed during the create pipeline, persisted, and exposed through the DTO.\n\n**New logic, each in its own small unit:**\n\n- **`OwnerRegistrationDays`** \u2014 a shared counter (mirroring the existing `OwnerCities` helper) that returns how many existing owners were registered on a given day. This is the \"single definition\" of grouping-by-day.\n- **`AssignOwnerBulkSignupWarning`** \u2014 a create-pipeline step that sets the flag `true` when more than 80 owners were *already* registered on the new owner's business day, else `false`. Runs after `BuildOwner`, before `SaveOwner`, mutating the built entity in place \u2014 same shape as `AssignOwnerNamesakeCount`.\n\n**Reuse instead of duplication:**\n\n- Refactored **`RejectDailyOwnerLimit`** to call the same `OwnerRegistrationDays.size(...)` helper (it had its own inline copy of the count loop). Now the daily-cap rule and the bulk-warning rule count a day identically \u2014 exactly what the test comment (\"shares the same accumulation path exercised by the daily-limit rule\") calls for.\n\n**Plumbing:**\n\n- `Owner` entity: `bulkSignupWarning` field + `bulk_signup_warning` column, added to all four `schema.sql` dialects.\n- `openapi.yml`: `bulkSignupWarning` boolean on the `Owner` schema \u2014 MapStruct maps it straight through by name (both create and GET responders reuse `toOwnerDto`).\n- `OwnerMapper.toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)`: ignore the new server-assigned field, matching the existing `customerCode`/`householdId`/`namesakeCount` ignores.\n- `owners.POST.yml`: inserted the `assignBulkSignupWarning` step (under `transaction` governance) between household assignment and save.\n\nThe test asserts the `false` (fresh-create) branch, which passes; the `>80` branch follows the same counting path the daily-limit rule already relies on. Full suite: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.",
